---
title: Text wrapping with float
---

# Wrapping content around an image

The `\{% float %}` container places an image on one side and flows the
following content around it. Unlike `\{% columns %}`, which lays out
independent side-by-side lanes, a float is a single flow: each block hugs
the image's near edge while it overlaps, then spans the full column once it
clears the image's bottom edge. The whole construct stays on one page — if
it will not fit, it moves to the next.

## Left float, with rich content

The wrap keeps its real structure — a paragraph reflows around the image,
but lists stay lists, code blocks stay code, and callouts stay callouts.

{% float width=170 %}
![](figure.svg)

A floated image sits on the chosen side while the content that follows it
wraps into the remaining width. This opening paragraph is narrow — every
line is shortened and shifted to clear the figure{% footnote %}Footnotes
raised inside a float are collected and numbered like any other, then
printed in the page's footnote pool.{% /footnote %} — then widens to the
full measure the moment it passes the image's bottom edge.

Structure survives the wrap rather than collapsing to plain prose:

* bullet lists stay lists,
* `inline code` keeps its monospace,
* and the markers are real.

{% callout type="info" %}
Callouts render beside the figure with their box, accent stripe and label
intact — not flattened into a paragraph.
{% /callout %}
{% /float %}

## Right float, wider image

{% float side="right" width=200 %}
![](figure.svg)

Set `side="right"` to move the image to the outer edge and wrap the content
down its left-hand side. The `width` attribute takes a fraction of the
column (a value up to 1), an explicit point value like this one, or a
percentage string such as `"45%"`.

The image never scales up beyond its natural size, matching how every other
image in the renderer behaves, so a small raster stays small and only a
vector or a large photo will fill the requested width.
{% /float %}

Ordinary full-width paragraphs resume here, clear of any float, so the
section ends on the normal measure.

## Multiple anchored floats (magazine)

Give each `\{% media %}` an explicit `side` and drop it *inline*, at the point
in the prose where it should anchor. The whole region becomes one continuous
text stream that wraps around every float — narrowing on the left past a
`side="left"` image, on the right before a `side="right"` one, and into the
channel between them where they overlap vertically. Full-width text returns
wherever no float is in the way.

{% float %}
Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Integer cursus
interdum {% media src="figure.svg" side="left" width=110 /%} dui, in gravida
ligula aliquam in. Vivamus vitae metus pharetra, ultricies metus quis,
consectetur augue. Phasellus ac mauris et nisi pretium aliquet sed ac orci.
Ut mi ipsum, mollis sed placerat et, bibendum et velit. Nunc vitae ornare leo.
Aliquam turpis sem, varius eget neque vel, mattis ultricies ex. Fusce metus
mauris {% media src="figure.svg" side="right" width=110 /%} fermentum at
porttitor quis, malesuada sed elit. Integer vel eros congue, volutpat nunc in,
lobortis ante. Curabitur pretium tincidunt lacus, nec ornare nisl. Trailing
text so the wrap closes back to the full measure once both figures clear.
{% /float %}

Prose-only: rich blocks between the floats are flattened to paragraphs — for
structured content beside a single image use the leading-image form above.
